WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
This beginner's DevOps course is your first&nbsp;
step toward a DevOps engineering role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:15.920
It is taught by the CEO and co founder of LayerCI.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:22.400
The goal of this course will be for regular&nbsp;
developers and regular engineering practitioners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.280
to learn fundamental DevOps concepts so that&nbsp;
they can go towards the DevOps engineering role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.480
We'll also be talking about DevOps&nbsp;
broadly in the introduction. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.200
beyond that will primarily be talking&nbsp;
about the engineering side of things.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.720
DevOps is a methodology that helps&nbsp;
engineering teams build products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:47.760
better by continuously integrating user feedback.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:54.240
And if you google DevOps, and you look for&nbsp;
pictures, you'll often see ones like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.080
it really helps understand how DevOps&nbsp;
is different than the traditional way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.240
of thinking about software developed.&nbsp;
Back in the day, software was developed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:07.360
much like things would be developed in a&nbsp;
factory. So any input would be programming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.840
and then the output, you'd have a product that&nbsp;
you could put on a CD, and you'd sell it to users.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.960
But since the advent of the Internet,&nbsp;
and have continuously updatable software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.960
it's become really easy to launch things and get&nbsp;
user feedback and integrate that into the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:28.400
product instead of making a new version of the&nbsp;
product. So websites like Facebook, continuously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.040
upgrade instead of requiring you to buy, you&nbsp;
know, a new version of Facebook, unlike, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:38.640
old games, like some city would require you to buy&nbsp;
a new version of SimCity. And that idea is really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:44.960
formalized by DevOps, the sections are planning,&nbsp;
where you take a set of features that you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.160
to build. And you work with your team to make some&nbsp;
specifications for those features might look like,

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.480
you code them. So developers on your team will&nbsp;
build out these features so that they can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:02.160
released. And of course, they're built. So for&nbsp;
a website, you might take the source code and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.600
bundle it into JavaScript that a user's browser&nbsp;
could run. For a video game, you might make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.240
releases for various different versions that&nbsp;
run on Linux and versions that run on Windows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.080
and versions that run in the browser. So you&nbsp;
take these built artifacts, and you test them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.920
So testing is both automatic and manual.&nbsp;
Automatic testing is usually colloquially known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:27.760
as continuous integration. And manual testing&nbsp;
is colloquially known as quality assurance, QA.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.440
And then after it's tested, and you know, the&nbsp;
stakeholders have all given their feedback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.880
it's released. And continuous deployment&nbsp;
strategies, releasing and deploying all happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.120
automatically after a change is known to be good.&nbsp;
There's a lot of automation that can be done here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:47.920
In larger teams. There's, you know, popular tools&nbsp;
like Spinnaker by netflix that we'll talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.720
in later talks. But the core idea is you want to&nbsp;
take the software, and you want to send it to your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:57.760
users in a way that they don't notice if there's&nbsp;
problems. So if there's an experimental UI change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.520
you might show it to a few percentage of&nbsp;
users and get their feedback before you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.040
show it broadly. Again, a company like Facebook,&nbsp;
which has billions of users, even if 1% of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:12.960
users complain, they'll get hundreds of millions&nbsp;
of emails, the release is built and deployed. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.240
deploying means it's released to your users for a&nbsp;
website, it would mean it's publicly accessible on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:24.000
the internet. For a CD ROM, you know, you bundle&nbsp;
your things onto a CD and you distribute that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.440
for a mobile release, you'd build the artifact&nbsp;
and you submit it to the App Store. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.960
the App Store would review it and then publish&nbsp;
a new update that your users could download.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.760
And then you operate it. Operating&nbsp;
is primarily things like scaling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.160
making sure that enough resources exist for&nbsp;
the load, adding more servers as required.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.640
configuring things dealing with&nbsp;
architectural problems, essentially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:53.040
on monitoring. So as your users use your software,&nbsp;
and especially as they submit things, and start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.520
jobs and create posts on your forums, you want&nbsp;
to make sure that those posts are all healthy.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.600
And then finally, you take all this feedback,&nbsp;
and you put it back to the planning stage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.480
So the planning stage takes all the user feedback&nbsp;
takes all of the things that the operations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.920
deployments, teams learned about deploying&nbsp;
and scaling the product. And then use that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:19.520
to build out new features, solve bugs, and make&nbsp;
new versions of the back end and new versions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:24.880
the architecture. And then just continues in the&nbsp;
cycle. And this is what people mean when they say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:29.680
our company uses DevOps, or our company is&nbsp;
tech forward, or our company is digitally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.560
transformed. They mean that instead of taking a&nbsp;
set of requirements and building one artifact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.200
which is then shipped, it's a continuous cycle&nbsp;
of taking feedback. You know, in these two weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:43.920
Scrum cycles, usually, and producing software&nbsp;
that users actually want to use that they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:51.120
had some say in producing DevOps engineering is&nbsp;
another common part of DevOps. So beyond just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:56.000
the methodology, which is something that maybe&nbsp;
the technical leaders and CEO would care about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.920
there's a subfield of DevOps engineering,&nbsp;
and this is usually what engineers mean.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:02.560
You know when they say DevOps, and&nbsp;
that's usually what job postings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.960
mean when they say DevOps. So if a job&nbsp;
posting is asking for a DevOps engineer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.840
you know, they're not asking for&nbsp;
someone that can plan and deploy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.280
code. They're mostly asking for someone that&nbsp;
can build a test, release, deploy and monitor.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:25.440
So the three pillars of DevOps engineering, our&nbsp;
pull request automation, deployment, automation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.800
and application performance management. And we'll&nbsp;
get into specifics about those. But the idea is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:36.720
pull request automation helps developers build&nbsp;
things faster, and helps them understand whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:42.960
their proposed change is good, faster. Deployment&nbsp;
automation, helps you deploy your code in a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.680
that users don't complain. Again, Facebook has&nbsp;
lots of deployment automation, because if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.760
just threw their code out into the void,&nbsp;
every time a developer made a change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:57.280
there'd be hundreds of millions of complaints. And&nbsp;
application performance management is automation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:01.600
around making sure that things are healthy. So&nbsp;
automatically detecting downtime, automatically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:05.920
waking someone up, if you know, the site goes&nbsp;
down overnight, automatically rolling back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.840
things if there's a problem. And we'll get into&nbsp;
the specifics of all of these in future talks.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.400
The first pillar, which I mentioned&nbsp;
was pull request automation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:21.280
has primarily to do with the developer feedback&nbsp;
cycle. So developers share work with each other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:26.400
by proposing these atomic sets of changes&nbsp;
called pull requests. And by atomic I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:29.760
they're full featured on their own,&nbsp;
they don't require other things to run.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:36.320
First, it's like if a developer proposes a pull&nbsp;
request, they should be expecting that that change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:41.200
is good. And as far as they can tell, the change&nbsp;
fulfills some business requirements. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:46.080
what they have to do is get through some gates.&nbsp;
So organizations and pull request automation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:50.960
their goal is to make sure that developers can&nbsp;
tell very quickly whether their change is good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:56.160
or not. So for example, if you're working on a&nbsp;
website, and the developer proposes a change that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:01.760
adds a typo, that's something that can easily&nbsp;
be automatically detected. And if you set up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:07.440
a typo gate that says no change may go in if it&nbsp;
contains a typo, that would be an easy way to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:12.560
sure that developers get automatic feedback about&nbsp;
their changes. People say pull requests, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:18.560
as of 2021, usually they mean Git. So Git is&nbsp;
a technology originally popularized by Linux,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:23.200
and it helps developers make these sorts of&nbsp;
changes and share them with each other. a pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:27.280
request is usually reviewed by at least one other&nbsp;
programmer and something called a code review,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:33.920
where the other programmer will tell you about&nbsp;
code style will tell the proposing programmer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:38.400
whether there's architectural problems, scaling&nbsp;
problems, subjective things that can't easily be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:44.320
automated. But that process of review can also be&nbsp;
greatly facilitated by a DevOps technology stack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:49.920
And DevOps automation can help with things like&nbsp;
ephemeral environments, and from linting. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:55.120
from all of these other automations that we'll&nbsp;
get into, after the code review has been done.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:00.880
Usually, an engineering manager or product manager&nbsp;
in charge of the functionality being proposed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:06.960
will get feedback. So if you create a new button,&nbsp;
on a website, you'd like the designer that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.560
designed the button, and you'd like the product&nbsp;
manager that requested the button be created.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:16.000
Both give feedback because if the button is&nbsp;
phrased poorly, if it's placed poorly, if it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:21.280
mobile, responsive, those are all problems that&nbsp;
would require another merge request. So it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:25.920
be great if the original merge request fulfilled&nbsp;
all of the requirements, the first time it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:31.280
proposed. And so usually, non technical people&nbsp;
will give feedback on pull requests as necessary.

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:39.840
So what can be automated for a DevOps engineer,

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:41.760
you can automate things like

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:48.160
automated test running, per change ephemeral&nbsp;
environments, automated security scanning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:54.160
notifications through reviewers, getting the&nbsp;
right people to review it at the right time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:58.880
And the end goal of all this automation is that&nbsp;
a developer should be able to propose a change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:03.840
and get it merged the same day they propose the&nbsp;
change. That's a huge organizational benefit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:10.240
because it means that critical bugs can be very&nbsp;
quickly fixed and merged and deployed without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:15.520
needing a special process. And it also means that&nbsp;
developers aren't bogged down in bureaucracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:19.680
they can propose changes once they get through&nbsp;
all the gates, the change will be deployed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:24.160
there isn't additional special gates that&nbsp;
they need to discover. So for example, if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:28.320
proper gates and automations have been set up,&nbsp;
a developer should be able to change a web page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:33.040
without having to ask everyone in the company.&nbsp;
Whether this web page is used in certain workflows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:38.880
or not. By virtue of passing the tests and passing&nbsp;
the QA review, it's assumed that the new change is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:43.360
good. And if a problem does arise, a new gate&nbsp;
can be added to the automation so that in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:49.120
future problems don't occur. The second pillar is&nbsp;
deployment automation. And the famous post from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:54.880
2000s, the founder of Stack Overflow, places Can&nbsp;
you make a build in one step as the second most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:59.760
important question for a development organization,&nbsp;
and things haven't really changed since then.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:04.960
The efficiency of the build process isn't the&nbsp;
only goal of deployment automation. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:10.000
other goals include the deployment strategies, I&nbsp;
talked about Canary deployments where you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:15.120
show a feature to one user at a time starting&nbsp;
new versions of your application without causing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:18.880
downtime. If you have to shut off your&nbsp;
website, before upgrading it, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:23.600
turning on the new version, the visitors that&nbsp;
visit the website in the middle of the upgrade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:27.600
will notice downtime. So there's clever&nbsp;
deployment strategies you can do to avoid that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:31.840
And finally, rolling back versions&nbsp;
in case something goes wrong.

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:37.440
It's easy to overcomplicate the planets. Many&nbsp;
companies have complex internal platforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:42.080
for building and distributing releases.&nbsp;
Broadly, success and deployment automation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:46.240
is finding the appropriate deployment tools to&nbsp;
fulfill business goals and configuring them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:49.782
And then ideal world there should be little&nbsp;
to no custom code for deploying. So off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.782 --> 00:10:54.960
shelf solutions like Spinnaker and harness are&nbsp;
wonderful places to start for this sort of thing.

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:02.880
Finally, application performance management, even&nbsp;
the best code can be hamstrung by operational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:08.720
errors. There's a famous case where a user put a&nbsp;
bunch of spaces at the end of their post in Stack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:12.800
Overflow. And they brought down Stack Overflow,&nbsp;
which is a very popular developer website,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:18.400
because Stack Overflow hadn't deployed their code&nbsp;
in a way that would deal well with a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:23.120
whitespace. So a bunch of space characters at the&nbsp;
end of a post, even with the best code, and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:27.120
with the simplest things like just a messaging&nbsp;
board, it's easy to have faults that make it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:32.720
production and are only uncovered by users. So&nbsp;
application performance management ensures that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:37.440
metrics like how long it's taking for requests&nbsp;
to be processed, how many servers are being used,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:43.600
all of those key health metrics are being&nbsp;
processed. And if there's a problem, like if all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:47.360
of the requests to the landing page are suddenly&nbsp;
taking a long time, the appropriate people can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:51.840
be notified automatically, instead of an engineer&nbsp;
discovering on Twitter that their website is down.

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:59.360
Logging. So as a program executes, it will&nbsp;
produce logs. And the logs generally have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:03.440
information about the state of things.&nbsp;
It's useful to be able to map back logs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:08.800
like, you know, a user visited the website with&nbsp;
information about that user. So what was their IP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:14.400
address? What was their username, what resource&nbsp;
did they access and what resources were used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:19.120
for fulfilling that access. So if they had to&nbsp;
load something from a database, and the database&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:25.040
was slow, it's useful to be able to say, the user&nbsp;
had a slow experience because their request was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:29.840
fulfilled slowly. But the request was fulfilled&nbsp;
slowly because it was fulfilled from the database&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:34.960
slowly. So mapping these requests all the way down&nbsp;
to their constituent components is very useful.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:41.840
monitoring. So again, I mentioned metrics&nbsp;
and automatically alerting people, but taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:48.880
the logs and metrics, how slower things how&nbsp;
much memory is left, and deciding what to do. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:54.240
if there's a bunch of load, you might decide&nbsp;
based on the metrics to automatically scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:59.360
the number of servers, so add more web servers&nbsp;
as they're being used. Based on the logs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:03.280
if there's errors, you might want to automatically&nbsp;
file tickets for engineers to look into them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:08.960
And if there's a downtime, you might want to call&nbsp;
someone the person on call so that they wake up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:13.040
and take care of the downtime. And they can drop&nbsp;
everything, they can have a pager, so to speak.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:19.440
And that's alerting. So alerting is when fault&nbsp;
is detected, some trigger has occurred based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:23.840
the metrics, some number of requests or to slow&nbsp;
things are unhealthy, you know, users are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:29.280
to notice degraded performance, someone should be&nbsp;
notified or something should be done some action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:34.160
a new product, shouldn't dive into DevOps,&nbsp;
engineering all at once. So all of that I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

