original developer has actually&nbsp;
tested that the change works.

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:53.840
So let's go back to our original&nbsp;
proposed change of changing the colors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:53.840 --> 00:31:59.840
in production from blue to purple.

00:32:03.760 --> 00:32:07.760
Here, we're going to make our&nbsp;
change and reopen the pull request.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.760 --> 00:32:12.080
But because we've configured a CI provider&nbsp;
for it, we'll be able to see that the tests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.080 --> 00:32:27.840
are running automatically directly&nbsp;
in the pull request view itself.

00:32:30.720 --> 00:32:35.520
Now, when our developer asks us for a review,&nbsp;
it'll be much easier for us to be able to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:35.520 --> 00:32:40.640
whether the change has negatively affected our&nbsp;
customers workflows. In particular, because we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:40.640 --> 00:32:46.960
configured Cypress and later ci to check that&nbsp;
logging in and posting messages still work. Well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:46.960 --> 00:32:52.880
know that for this change, even though many files&nbsp;
might have been changed, the core workflows still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.880 --> 00:32:57.280
work, which gives us a degree of confidence that&nbsp;
nothing terribly bad has happened with the code.

00:33:01.680 --> 00:33:05.520
So we can look at the file change for our&nbsp;
first idea of what the developer has done.

00:33:07.600 --> 00:33:11.920
And then we can view what the CI is doing.&nbsp;
So if we open the relevant pipeline,

00:33:13.440 --> 00:33:17.360
we'll see that the tests are in progress of&nbsp;
running, the new version of the application has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:23.520
been built and started within the CI runner.&nbsp;
And the tests are running one by one. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.520 --> 00:33:28.480
it's tested that you can post chat messages&nbsp;
within our alternative slacks chat page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:32.080
the landing page loads, and&nbsp;
logging in works correctly.

00:33:35.520 --> 00:33:40.080
So now within our pull request view, we'll be&nbsp;
able to see a big checkmark here, which shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:40.080 --> 00:33:45.920
that all of the relevant ci checks have passed.&nbsp;
And then you can even automate within GitHub or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:45.920 --> 00:33:50.720
other source code management platforms that&nbsp;
certain checks must pass entirely. So you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:50.720 --> 00:33:56.000
automate that all ci checks must pass before&nbsp;
a change could be merged. Let's make sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:56.000 --> 00:34:00.480
developers are never reviewing code that's so&nbsp;
obviously broken that it's breaking your tests.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:01.520 --> 00:34:06.160
And you don't only have to run end to end tests&nbsp;
here. You can also run linters unit tests and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:06.160 --> 00:34:11.120
other versions of tests, which we talked about&nbsp;
throughout this series of talks. And now that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:11.120 --> 00:34:16.080
I'm happy with the change, I've reviewed&nbsp;
the files, and I see that the CI has passed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:20.080
I can merge it with a great deal more confidence&nbsp;
than if I didn't have this automation in place.

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:24.080
That's it for setting up ci and an applied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:24.080 --> 00:34:33.840
setting. Let's get back to&nbsp;
theory for a little bit.

00:34:51.200 --> 00:34:54.480
Continuing on the topic of testing&nbsp;
and continuous integration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:54.480 --> 00:35:00.000
let's talk about code coverage. So code coverage&nbsp;
quantitatively measures how comprehensive

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:04.960
The tests for a code base are, you might think&nbsp;
that you have enough tests to find all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:04.960 --> 00:35:09.120
common bugs and to really check all of the&nbsp;
functionality of your app. But it's hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:09.120 --> 00:35:12.960
put a number on it. Unless you're measuring&nbsp;
code coverage. This is what a code coverage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:12.960 --> 00:35:16.800
graph looks like from a popular tool.&nbsp;
Each of these squares represents a file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:22.800
and the color represents how many tests are&nbsp;
covering that file. So bright green means 100%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:27.920
of the file is tested. And bright red means&nbsp;
none of the file is tested. So that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:27.920 --> 00:35:31.760
be a priority of a file that should either&nbsp;
be tested or excluded from the measurement.

00:35:34.480 --> 00:35:36.880
So let's say you're taking&nbsp;
over an existing code base,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:37.440 --> 00:35:40.000
it's relatively large at 100,000 lines of code.

00:35:41.360 --> 00:35:47.280
Over the years, it's been adopted by a couple&nbsp;
100 users, and you're expected to maintain it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:53.120
and add features without harming those users. So&nbsp;
the first place you look at is the unit tests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:53.120 --> 00:35:57.440
which we discussed earlier. But they weren't&nbsp;
really prioritized by the previous maintainers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:57.440 --> 00:36:01.280
So there's a mismatch of libraries and naming&nbsp;
conventions. And it's kind of hard to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:06.240
which tests are testing which files&nbsp;
and which files need to be tested.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:07.200 --> 00:36:10.800
And before you write any new features,&nbsp;
you'd like an objective way to measure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:11.600 --> 00:36:16.800
how sensitive certain parts of the codebase are to&nbsp;
being changed. If something has very comprehensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:22.160
tests, you'll be much less scared to make changes&nbsp;
and add features that touch that part of the code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:22.160 --> 00:36:27.280
than if there's a part of the code that doesn't&nbsp;
have pests. So this is where code coverage really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:27.280 --> 00:36:33.520
shines. You've got a complicated code base that&nbsp;
has existing users, you'd like to enforce that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.520 --> 00:36:39.600
tests are written so that things aren't broken&nbsp;
in an objective way. So getting into the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:39.600 --> 00:36:44.400
code of this whole series, let's look at this&nbsp;
JavaScript function, which I will make bigger.

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:49.520
So this is a very simple&nbsp;
function, if not a bit contrived.

00:36:50.800 --> 00:36:52.000
It takes a number.

00:36:53.200 --> 00:37:02.560
And it defines a few variables. It loops up to&nbsp;
that number, pushing strings into a results list.

00:37:04.000 --> 00:37:08.160
And then every 50 elements, it pushes&nbsp;
a special string into the results list.

00:37:09.520 --> 00:37:15.840
So this whole function is 10 lines of&nbsp;
code. But not all 10 lines are equal. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:16.720 --> 00:37:22.400
really, there's three kinds of lines in a&nbsp;
program like this. There's the syntax lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:22.400 --> 00:37:26.720
like these closing ones that don't actually&nbsp;
have any code in them. They're simply syntactic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:27.520 --> 00:37:32.080
constructs for the programmers benefit.&nbsp;
They don't you know, it doesn't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:32.080 --> 00:37:36.240
make sense to test these because how would&nbsp;
you test that a semi colon existed or not?

00:37:38.160 --> 00:37:42.880
There's logic lines like this one, which&nbsp;
actually have side effects. And by side effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:42.880 --> 00:37:46.880
I mean that these lines, if you remove them,&nbsp;
would change the behavior of the program.

00:37:48.720 --> 00:37:52.560
And those branch lines like this one,&nbsp;
which changed the flow of the program.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:53.120 --> 00:38:00.160
So for loops, and if statements in programming are&nbsp;
used, as constructs that change the order of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:04.160
commands that run, so this if&nbsp;
statement, if it evaluates to true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:04.720 --> 00:38:10.400
would run this line. And if it didn't evaluate to&nbsp;
true, it wouldn't run this line. So to reiterate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:10.400 --> 00:38:18.800
the three kinds of lines are syntactical ones that&nbsp;
don't do anything. The actual logic ones that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:24.000
effects, and the branch ones that&nbsp;
change which lines of code execute.

00:38:27.680 --> 00:38:33.360
And code coverage is usually defined&nbsp;
as line coverage. So it's the ratio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:33.360 --> 00:38:40.240
of the non syntax lines which are executed by&nbsp;
tests over the total number of non syntax lines.

00:38:41.520 --> 00:38:42.960
So again, consider this test.

00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:49.440
If you expect the function&nbsp;
should work with the input to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:53.200
and you manually calculate what the&nbsp;
function should return for the input to

00:38:54.640 --> 00:38:56.480
this would be a unit test for your function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:57.760 --> 00:39:02.640
But since you're only executing it on the input&nbsp;
to this if statement, which requires an input&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:10.800
of at least 50 to execute, wouldn't run. So you'd&nbsp;
be testing this line, it would execute this line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.800 --> 00:39:15.600
it would execute and this line, which&nbsp;
would also execute, so you'd be executing

00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:19.040
five out of six lines,

00:39:20.800 --> 00:39:26.240
and the deed that would be 83% test&nbsp;
coverage. So just the single test gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:26.240 --> 00:39:31.200
us most of the way to understanding our&nbsp;
function and understanding its problems.

00:39:33.440 --> 00:39:38.720
related concept is called branch coverage. So&nbsp;
instead of measuring how many lines of code it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:38.720 --> 00:39:44.480
measures groups of lines, in our example,&nbsp;
above, there's only two branches. There is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:44.480 --> 00:39:53.600
the main branch. There's the body of the for&nbsp;
loop, and there's actually a third branch called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:53.600 --> 00:40:00.080
The if statement body. So here at this line&nbsp;
will always execute the body of the for loop.

00:40:00.080 --> 00:40:07.840
will only execute if i is less than n. so here&nbsp;
you need and to be greater than or equal to one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:07.840 --> 00:40:12.880
or these lines to execute. And this line will&nbsp;
only execute if i is greater than or equal to 49.

00:40:14.320 --> 00:40:20.880
And so branch coverage would be how many&nbsp;
individual branches out of these three are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:20.880 --> 00:40:27.040
evaluated to true by a test. So you'd like&nbsp;
to know how many of all of the branches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.680 --> 00:40:33.040
are tested. And this is useful because if this&nbsp;
line of code executes, then this line of code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:33.040 --> 00:40:38.400
will always execute. So treating them both as&nbsp;
individual things that need to be tested, doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.400 --> 00:40:43.680
really mean as much as taking the bodies of these&nbsp;
statements as things that need to be tested.

00:40:47.600 --> 00:40:50.320
And if you measured the test with branch coverage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:50.320 --> 00:40:54.800
you'd see the two of the three branches&nbsp;
or x evaluated during the test.

00:40:56.240 --> 00:41:01.360
So when should you care about this line coverage&nbsp;
in branch coverage, we've already discussed one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:01.360 --> 00:41:06.480
scenario where you've inherited an existing code&nbsp;
base. However, it's important in many different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:06.480 --> 00:41:10.800
situations. In general, you should measure&nbsp;
and optimize for code coverage. If any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:10.800 --> 00:41:15.680
the following are true. Your product has users.&nbsp;
And those users might leave if they're affected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:15.680 --> 00:41:20.560
by bugs, in which case, it's important to&nbsp;
measure code coverage because it lets you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:20.560 --> 00:41:25.920
work with your team to improve the code coverage&nbsp;
and reduce the number of bugs. You're working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:25.920 --> 00:41:29.760
with developers that aren't immediately&nbsp;
trustworthy, like contractors or insurance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:30.400 --> 00:41:35.440
that you're bringing them into your code base,&nbsp;
they need to make changes in some fixed timescale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:35.440 --> 00:41:40.320
like form up internship. So they can immediately&nbsp;
become experts in the entire code base.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:41.120 --> 00:41:44.880
And you'd like them to be able to make changes&nbsp;
without worrying too much about things breaking.

00:41:46.080 --> 00:41:50.240
Or, if you're working on a very large code&nbsp;
bases, many individually testable components.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:54.880
Your code coverage analysis can complement test&nbsp;
driven development, which we talked about in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:54.880 --> 00:42:00.160
previous talk, to make sure that everyone on the&nbsp;
team is generally working on important things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:00.160 --> 00:42:02.720
and that the things they make&nbsp;
won't break in the future.

00:42:04.560 --> 00:42:08.880
So it's a common mistake in code review automation&nbsp;
to make things too rigid before the product has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:08.880 --> 00:42:14.240
enough users, if you force developers to get&nbsp;
100% branch coverage, so to write two to five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:14.240 --> 00:42:18.960
unit tests for every function, it's going to&nbsp;
make them much slower at developing features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:18.960 --> 00:42:23.520
that users will actually notice. Remember&nbsp;
that tests are never viewed by users.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:24.160 --> 00:42:28.960
So the only thing that users care about is the&nbsp;
stability of the system. So if you have an MVP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:28.960 --> 00:42:32.560
or if you have a product that doesn't have very&nbsp;
many active users, yet, it might not be worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:32.560 --> 00:42:39.280
it to measure or optimized for branch coverage.&nbsp;
until those users care a lot about stability.

00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:45.680
And by writing unit tests, and other types of&nbsp;
tests, an important thing to keep in mind is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:45.680 --> 00:42:49.680
developers are solidifying the implementations&nbsp;
of features that they might have to throw out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:53.680
If you build a feature, and it ends up not&nbsp;
being something that your users actually want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.240 --> 00:42:59.280
it's always a better idea to throw out&nbsp;
that feature, then build it long term. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:59.280 --> 00:43:03.520
if a developer builds a feature, and writes many&nbsp;
tests for it to improve the code coverage of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:03.520 --> 00:43:08.560
feature, they'll be much more likely not to throw&nbsp;
it out, because they'll feel a sense of ownership.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:08.560 --> 00:43:14.000
And they'll feel a sense of sunk cost in having&nbsp;
built this feature and made it good, so to speak.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:14.000 --> 00:43:18.000
So it's important not to over optimize for&nbsp;
these things before, they're important. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.000 --> 00:43:23.200
it's a subjective idea. But really, you'll notice&nbsp;
when your users start complaining about stability.

00:43:25.760 --> 00:43:29.600
So organizationally, there are some&nbsp;
common policies related to code coverage.

00:43:32.240 --> 00:43:36.880
The first one is useful when you inherit a code&nbsp;
base. And the policy is that code coverage must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:36.880 --> 00:43:41.920
not decrease. This one is one of the easiest ones&nbsp;
to automate. It's especially useful if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:41.920 --> 00:43:47.360
taking over an existing code base, as I mentioned.&nbsp;
And the idea is that code coverage ratio should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:47.360 --> 00:43:54.160
never decrease. If the current code has 75% of&nbsp;
its lines tested, and your new change introduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:54.160 --> 00:44:00.480
40 lines of code, at least 30 of those lines&nbsp;
will need to be tested. Otherwise, your changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:00.480 --> 00:44:07.840
code coverage would be less than 75% 30 out of 40.&nbsp;
And you'd be decreasing the average code coverage.

00:44:08.400 --> 00:44:12.640
As with most code coverage policies, this will&nbsp;
increase stability, so there'll be less bugs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:12.640 --> 00:44:16.960
because things will be better tested at the&nbsp;
expense of developer speed. So developers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:16.960 --> 00:44:20.480
will have to make some complicated tests,&nbsp;
and they might spend a lot of time making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:20.480 --> 00:44:24.880
testing infrastructure. So features will be&nbsp;
shipped less quickly if you make this sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:30.080
policy. And if you enforce it&nbsp;
with code review automation.

00:44:32.480 --> 00:44:36.560
unfortunate side effect of this policy is&nbsp;
that changes that would be harder to test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:36.560 --> 00:44:40.320
such as integrations will be less&nbsp;
likely to be worked on by developers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:40.880 --> 00:44:44.960
So developers are incentivized by&nbsp;
their paycheck and by their manager to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:45.760 --> 00:44:50.800
ship features quickly to make many features&nbsp;
per Scrum cycle. And so if certain features are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:50.800 --> 00:44:55.040
harder to test, because they require internet&nbsp;
connectivity, or connected third party API's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:56.000 --> 00:45:00.000
those features will be harder to make and&nbsp;
harder to test. And so developers will be

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:04.240
less likely to make them regardless of&nbsp;
whether they're important to the users or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:04.240 --> 00:45:09.520
So it might be useful to have an exemptions policy&nbsp;
in place for things like third party integrations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:09.520 --> 00:45:14.320
If your organization decides to go for this&nbsp;
code coverage must not decrease policy.

00:45:17.040 --> 00:45:22.480
Another useful policy is code owners for test&nbsp;
files. If you've used code coverage automation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:22.480 --> 00:45:28.400
to keep code well tested, it's often beneficial to&nbsp;
define code owners for the tests themselves. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:28.400 --> 00:45:33.520
means that developers can change implementation&nbsp;
details without formal reviewers. But logic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:33.520 --> 00:45:42.080
changes. So the tests define what success means&nbsp;
for a function or for an algorithm, then, changing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:42.080 --> 00:45:46.960
the tests for a new implementation would need&nbsp;
to be approved by a senior developer or manager.

00:45:48.320 --> 00:45:55.360
in GitHub, with an engineering manager, a GitHub&nbsp;
code owners file might contain this, which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:55.360 --> 00:46:03.760
that spec dot j s is a common JavaScript testing,&nbsp;
naming convention. And at engineering manager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:03.760 --> 00:46:09.280
username means if, if there's a file called code&nbsp;
owners, which contains this, then the engineering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:09.280 --> 00:46:14.320
manager will need to approve any change, which&nbsp;
changes a test, which is probably a good policy.

00:46:17.760 --> 00:46:21.120
So if you're working in a large code base&nbsp;
with test driven development, especially,

00:46:22.480 --> 00:46:27.200
or if you're hiring interns, or contractors, or if&nbsp;
your users are especially sensitive to bugs, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:27.200 --> 00:46:31.200
you're afraid that they'll have a bad experience,&nbsp;
if the even small bugs make it to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:34.960
it might be in your team's best interest to&nbsp;
install a code coverage measurement tool.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:35.920 --> 00:46:39.280
And at the time of writing, these&nbsp;
are the three most common ones in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:39.280 --> 00:46:55.840
the open source world code coverage,&nbsp;
coveralls and Code Climate.

00:46:56.720 --> 00:47:00.560
So we've talked about testing, and we've talked&nbsp;
about continuous integration. And those are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:00.560 --> 00:47:05.600
really like the initial things that are set up&nbsp;
in a DevOps code review automation pipeline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:09.920
But the problem is that it requires the&nbsp;
developers to be on board. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:09.920 --> 00:47:13.680
developers are probably busy building&nbsp;
features and might not necessarily want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:14.400 --> 00:47:18.320
make tests or improve test coverage.&nbsp;
So let's talk about linting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:18.320 --> 00:47:23.840
which is something that approximates testing, but&nbsp;
doesn't need the developers to spend any time.

00:47:26.240 --> 00:47:31.760
linters are programs that look at a program source&nbsp;
code, and find problems automatically. They're a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:31.760 --> 00:47:36.560
common feature of pull request automation, because&nbsp;
they ensure that obvious bugs do not make it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:36.560 --> 00:47:42.960
production, obvious here in quotes. So an example&nbsp;
of linting. Let's again, look at a JavaScript&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:42.960 --> 00:47:46.640
program, the very simple one, you should&nbsp;
understand even if you don't know JavaScript,

00:47:47.920 --> 00:47:53.840
it defines a variable var x equals five,&nbsp;
and defines a function but continues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:54.880 --> 00:48:00.880
after the open bracket, which is generally&nbsp;
considered bad practice. It uses let for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:00.880 --> 00:48:05.520
second variable and defines it with the same name&nbsp;
as the first one. So this is just confusing, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:06.240 --> 00:48:09.920
you know, wouldn't be called good code, a code&nbsp;
reviewer would mentioned this in a code review.

00:48:11.200 --> 00:48:16.240
And then it says while x is less than 100,&nbsp;
console, log x, and then it closes the while loop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:16.240 --> 00:48:21.520
on this line, and it messes up the end Det. These&nbsp;
three lines should be indented for consistency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:22.320 --> 00:48:27.200
and then it closes the function. Finally, you&nbsp;
should realize that this while loop goes forever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:27.760 --> 00:48:31.680
x isn't incremented in the body of the while&nbsp;
loop. So just by looking at the code statically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:31.680 --> 00:48:37.280
without running any environment or looking&nbsp;
at the code with a browser even, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:37.280 --> 00:48:40.800
tell that this loop will run forever, and that's&nbsp;
probably something a programmer didn't intend.

00:48:43.120 --> 00:48:43.280
So

00:48:45.040 --> 00:48:51.840
much of this feedback could be automated, a set&nbsp;
of rules like don't shadow variables. Never name&nbsp;&nbsp;

