memory for the operating system itself. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:36.560 --> 01:21:40.480
containers don't need all of the operating system&nbsp;
because it's the processes that are being lied to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:41.200 --> 01:21:45.200
So VMs use more memory, they're&nbsp;
slower, and they need more storage.

01:21:46.400 --> 01:21:50.480
So given these performance benefits, it looks&nbsp;
like containers are almost always a better choice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:51.200 --> 01:21:53.200
And in most cases they are However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:53.200 --> 01:21:57.600
there's a few cases where VMs are a better&nbsp;
choice. Again, VMs mean virtual machines.

01:21:59.520 --> 01:22:04.400
If you run in untrusted, so user supplied&nbsp;
code, it's difficult to be confident that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:04.400 --> 01:22:07.920
they can't escape a container. This&nbsp;
has gotten better in recent years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:07.920 --> 01:22:13.520
but it's long been a contentious point. Virtual&nbsp;
Machines are much older and much more mature. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:13.520 --> 01:22:17.040
if you're running untrusted code, usually it's&nbsp;
a good idea to put that within a container.

01:22:18.560 --> 01:22:23.520
If you're running a Windows or Mac OS script,&nbsp;
so you're running a script that only runs on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:23.520 --> 01:22:28.080
another operating system, you'd need to use a&nbsp;
VM for similar reasons. Or if you're running&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:28.080 --> 01:22:31.760
an old video game that doesn't run in Linux,&nbsp;
and you'd like to run it on a Linux computer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:31.760 --> 01:22:35.680
you'd need to VM. And vice versa, if you're&nbsp;
using VMs, and other operating systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:35.680 --> 01:22:40.480
if you'd like to run a Linux program in Windows,&nbsp;
you'd usually have to use a VM to run it.

01:22:41.840 --> 01:22:45.280
And finally, you can emulate hardware&nbsp;
devices like graphics cards with a VM.

01:22:46.480 --> 01:22:51.680
So if you're testing that your graphics card&nbsp;
works correctly, you could emulate the response&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:51.680 --> 01:22:55.360
that it would give and then test that the&nbsp;
operating system is working as expected.

01:22:56.720 --> 01:22:59.120
So that's the big difference&nbsp;
between VMs and containers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:59.120 --> 01:23:01.120
And these are really the two&nbsp;
things that you often deploy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:01.680 --> 01:23:05.840
So let's go into actual deployment strategies&nbsp;
in the next talk. I'll see you there.

01:23:20.320 --> 01:23:26.000
Let's keep talking about deployments. So rolling&nbsp;
deployments are one of the most popular deployment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:26.000 --> 01:23:30.160
strategies. And we'll talk about the pros and cons&nbsp;
of different deployment strategies throughout this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:30.160 --> 01:23:33.840
section. Rolling deployments themselves work&nbsp;
by starting a new version of the application,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:34.400 --> 01:23:38.960
sending traffic to the new version, to make sure&nbsp;
everything's okay, and then showing off the old&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:38.960 --> 01:23:43.920
version and repeating that until all versions of&nbsp;
the old version or versions of the new version.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:44.640 --> 01:23:46.720
I realize I've said version many times. So let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:46.720 --> 01:23:49.120
look at pictures that will&nbsp;
help illustrate the point.

01:23:50.720 --> 01:23:58.400
This is the myrn app. myrn stands for&nbsp;
MongoDB, Node JS, react, and express js. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:58.400 --> 01:24:03.200
the user's web browser connects to&nbsp;
both the front end and the back end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:03.200 --> 01:24:07.600
where front end is the stuff that the user&nbsp;
sees. And back end is the services that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:07.600 --> 01:24:12.160
provide connections to the database. So if&nbsp;
you log in, you're connecting to the back end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:12.160 --> 01:24:14.800
If you're just viewing the landing page,&nbsp;
you're connecting to the front end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:15.840 --> 01:24:20.720
Let's say your app has enough traffic that users&nbsp;
will notice if it goes down for a little while.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:21.600 --> 01:24:25.520
How would you push a new version of the&nbsp;
application without causing downtime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:25.520 --> 01:24:30.080
This is where rolling deployments come in. The&nbsp;
high level algorithm for a rolling deployment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:30.080 --> 01:24:36.560
looks like this. So you create an instance of&nbsp;
the new version of the backend say, you wait&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:36.560 --> 01:24:41.440
until it's up. So you keep trying to connect to&nbsp;
it until you get a response that satisfactory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:42.160 --> 01:24:46.080
And then you delete an old version and&nbsp;
route the traffic to the new version.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:47.120 --> 01:24:51.680
If any instances of the old version still&nbsp;
exist, go back to step one and repeat.

01:24:52.960 --> 01:24:56.400
And our myrn example, we'd initially&nbsp;
see three instances of the initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:56.400 --> 01:25:00.240
version and one instance of the new&nbsp;
version. And we'd repeat the process

01:25:00.240 --> 01:25:04.240
Until we had three instances of the new version&nbsp;
and one instance of the initial version.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:05.120 --> 01:25:09.680
So here, all of the versions are the back end.

01:25:11.200 --> 01:25:19.520
And we add a new version of the new back end. And&nbsp;
we turn off a version of the old back end. And we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:19.520 --> 01:25:25.920
keep repeating that. So as time goes on, the red&nbsp;
ones replaced the pink ones. And then after a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:25.920 --> 01:25:30.240
loops of this, the only ones remaining are red&nbsp;
ones, we've added a red one, remove the pink one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:30.240 --> 01:25:34.240
added the red one, remove the pink one. And we're&nbsp;
red is the newest version of the application.

01:25:35.760 --> 01:25:40.800
So what are the benefits of rolling deployments&nbsp;
over other ways of deploying things? Well, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:40.800 --> 01:25:45.680
well supported. Rolling deployments are relatively&nbsp;
straightforward to implement. In most cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:45.680 --> 01:25:49.200
they're natively supported. And several&nbsp;
orchestrators, if you've heard of Kubernetes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:49.200 --> 01:25:54.320
for example, Kubernetes helps you with&nbsp;
this. AWS, Amazon Elastic Beanstalk&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:54.320 --> 01:25:56.800
also supports rolling deployments.

01:25:58.320 --> 01:26:02.480
They don't have huge bursts. So in another&nbsp;
deployment strategy, which we'll talk about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:03.120 --> 01:26:09.200
if you had three versions of the back end, you&nbsp;
need to start six in total, to deploy the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:09.200 --> 01:26:14.080
version, and then you turn off the old three.&nbsp;
So doubles for the duration of the deployments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:14.080 --> 01:26:17.200
the amount of things running, which might&nbsp;
be difficult if you have a finite number&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:17.200 --> 01:26:23.200
of servers, for example. It's also not&nbsp;
uncommon for services like databases&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:23.200 --> 01:26:28.560
to limit the amount of connections. So if you&nbsp;
had six versions of the backend connecting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:28.560 --> 01:26:32.320
the database, now that might be too much load&nbsp;
on the database, so that could cause problems.

01:26:33.440 --> 01:26:36.560
And really, deployments are easily reverted. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:36.560 --> 01:26:40.480
in the course of an upgrade, you notice&nbsp;
problems, it's usually easy to reverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:40.480 --> 01:26:44.400
the rolling deployment by just going in the&nbsp;
opposite direction, removing a red adding a pink,

01:26:45.600 --> 01:26:48.720
you can go in the opposite direction as&nbsp;
well to rollback which is an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:48.720 --> 01:26:51.120
characteristic of deploying&nbsp;
because things always go wrong.

01:26:52.560 --> 01:26:58.240
The downsides of rolling deployments are they&nbsp;
can be slow to run. So if you have 100 replicas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:58.240 --> 01:27:03.840
and you're replacing one at a time, and it&nbsp;
takes 20 seconds, it would take 2000 seconds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:04.400 --> 01:27:08.000
to replace all of the versions, which&nbsp;
is quite a long time for deployment.

01:27:09.200 --> 01:27:14.080
This can be mitigated by increasing the number of&nbsp;
services being turned on and shut off at a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:14.080 --> 01:27:17.840
which is sometimes called a burst&nbsp;
limit, or a rolling deployment size.

01:27:19.520 --> 01:27:23.440
The other problem is API compatibility, which&nbsp;
is the biggest problem of rolling deployments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:24.240 --> 01:27:29.920
So if you add a new version of an API endpoint to&nbsp;
your back end, and consume it in your front end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:30.480 --> 01:27:35.040
then since you're not switching them both at&nbsp;
the same time, you might have version one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:35.040 --> 01:27:40.480
your back end serving a request for version two of&nbsp;
your front end. And then that API wouldn't exist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:40.480 --> 01:27:44.080
so there'd be errors visible to the user for&nbsp;
the duration of the deployment. This can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:44.080 --> 01:27:48.400
mitigated with complicated routing techniques,&nbsp;
but it's generally better to make API is backwards&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:48.400 --> 01:27:53.840
compatible. So make version two of the front end&nbsp;
be compatible with version one of the back end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:54.480 --> 01:27:58.240
So rolling deployments are relatively simple&nbsp;
to understand and generally well supported.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:59.040 --> 01:28:02.320
If your users mind when there's&nbsp;
downtime, it's an excellent first step&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:02.320 --> 01:28:07.120
to deploy using a rolling deployment strategy.&nbsp;
The key programming consideration is to ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:07.120 --> 01:28:12.480
that services can consume both the old version&nbsp;
and the new version of services API's. If this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:12.480 --> 01:28:17.200
contract was violated, users might see errors&nbsp;
for the duration of the deployments. Let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:17.200 --> 01:28:21.280
talk more about deployment strategies.&nbsp;
And we'll go into bluegreen deployments.

01:28:35.200 --> 01:28:38.560
Another deployment strategy people&nbsp;
often see are bluegreen deployments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:39.280 --> 01:28:43.120
To set up a bluegreen. Deployment teams&nbsp;
need to disambiguate which services will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:43.120 --> 01:28:47.680
consistently deployed, and which services will&nbsp;
be shared across versions of the application.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:47.680 --> 01:28:52.240
I'll explain a little bit more of what I mean by&nbsp;
that in the next section, a database server would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:52.240 --> 01:28:56.960
be a shared resource, multiple versions of the&nbsp;
app would connect to the server at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:56.960 --> 01:29:00.480
and a standard deployment would generally&nbsp;
not upgrade or modify the database.

01:29:01.680 --> 01:29:05.520
In our mern example, all of the&nbsp;
other services or cluster resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:06.240 --> 01:29:10.000
new versions of them would be&nbsp;
deployed on every prod push.

01:29:10.000 --> 01:29:15.600
So in a bluegreen deployment strategy, where&nbsp;
you're upgrading the JavaScript or the myrn app,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:15.600 --> 01:29:20.320
this is what that would look like. So there's&nbsp;
a blue version, and a green version of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:20.320 --> 01:29:25.840
application where each is a fully standalone&nbsp;
stack. But we're each connects to a shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:25.840 --> 01:29:30.560
database, and the database is not part of blue&nbsp;
or green. It's a shared resource used by both&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:30.560 --> 01:29:35.360
bluegreen deployments are so called because they&nbsp;
maintain two separate clusters, one named blue and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:35.360 --> 01:29:41.120
one named green out of convention. If the current&nbsp;
version of the application is deployed to blue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:41.760 --> 01:29:45.440
we deploy the new version to green&nbsp;
and use it as a staging environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:45.440 --> 01:29:49.280
to ensure that the new version of the app&nbsp;
works correctly before setting users to it.

01:29:50.240 --> 01:29:53.280
After we're confident that the new&nbsp;
version of the software works correctly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:53.280 --> 01:29:55.840
we'd move over production load from blue to green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:56.400 --> 01:30:00.000
and then repeat the cycle in&nbsp;
the opposite direction. So here

01:30:00.720 --> 01:30:06.240
We started with the users being sent to blue,&nbsp;
which contains version one of the application. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:06.240 --> 01:30:10.720
then we're investigating that version two works.&nbsp;
And after we're certain that version two works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:11.640 --> 01:30:16.000
will route users to version two. And&nbsp;
then the version one is unused. And we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:16.000 --> 01:30:18.560
can shut it off and replace it with version three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:18.560 --> 01:30:23.520
make sure it works, and then switch user traffic&nbsp;
over to it over and over again. On the benefits&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:23.520 --> 01:30:27.920
side, bluegreen deployments are conceptually&nbsp;
very easy to understand. To set them up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:27.920 --> 01:30:32.960
you just have to create two identical production&nbsp;
environments and send requests to either one or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:32.960 --> 01:30:37.280
the other, which is relatively simple with&nbsp;
services like Amazon elastic load balancing.

01:30:38.720 --> 01:30:42.560
They're also quite powerful, longer running&nbsp;
tasks, like downloads can continue running&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:42.560 --> 01:30:46.800
in the old version of the application after&nbsp;
traffic is switched over to the new version.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:47.360 --> 01:30:49.840
So if a user has an established&nbsp;
connection to green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:50.480 --> 01:30:54.960
and you've switched everyone else over to blue,&nbsp;
that connection can continue finishing whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:54.960 --> 01:31:00.160
it was doing. So if you're watching video, and&nbsp;
you'd like to download it entirely to the user,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:00.160 --> 01:31:05.440
which might take minutes that can continue&nbsp;
going even during a prod push. Additionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:05.440 --> 01:31:08.800
bluegreen deployments can be extended to many&nbsp;
different workflows, which we'll discuss.

01:31:10.880 --> 01:31:15.600
There's a few notable drawbacks to bluegreen&nbsp;
deployments, it's difficult to deploy a hotfix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:15.600 --> 01:31:20.480
for example, to revert a change, because the&nbsp;
old cluster might be running longer running&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:20.480 --> 01:31:26.080
tasks and unavailable to switch to. So if&nbsp;
you have version one of the application,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:26.080 --> 01:31:30.560
you switch over to version two, you realize&nbsp;
version two is having problems, you might want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:30.560 --> 01:31:35.120
push version three very quickly, which addresses&nbsp;
those problems. But version one would be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:35.120 --> 01:31:40.320
only place you could deploy version three. So you&nbsp;
wouldn't be able to do that. It's also finicky to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:40.320 --> 01:31:45.440
transfer load between the clusters, if resources&nbsp;
autoscale, which we'll talk about later on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:45.440 --> 01:31:49.040
and load is transferred all at once the&nbsp;
new cluster might not have enough resources&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:49.040 --> 01:31:54.880
allocated to serve the surge of requests, because&nbsp;
requests went all at once to peak production load.

01:31:56.160 --> 01:32:00.160
And finally, if one cluster modifies the&nbsp;
shared service, like adding a column to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:00.160 --> 01:32:04.560
table in the database, it may affect the other&nbsp;
cluster despite it not being the live one.

01:32:08.080 --> 01:32:12.240
So here's some common extensions to&nbsp;
bluegreen deployments. As I mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:12.240 --> 01:32:17.040
they're very extensible. And many teams set up&nbsp;
advanced workflows around bluegreen deployments&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:17.040 --> 01:32:22.080
to improve stability and deployment velocity. The&nbsp;
first idea is a natural extension of bluegreen&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:22.080 --> 01:32:25.840
deployments, which I call rainbow deployments, but&nbsp;
I don't think there's a standard term for them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:26.480 --> 01:32:30.560
Instead of only having two clusters, some&nbsp;
teams keep an arbitrary number of clusters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:30.560 --> 01:32:36.800
so blue, green, red, yellow, so on. This is useful&nbsp;
when you're running very long running tasks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:38.000 --> 01:32:42.640
If you're working on a distributed web scraper,&nbsp;
and you're scraping tasks take days, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:42.640 --> 01:32:46.560
you might need your clusters to&nbsp;
last until the last job is finished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:46.560 --> 01:32:51.600
to ensure things continue working as&nbsp;
expected. So with the rainbow deployments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:51.600 --> 01:32:55.840
you'd keep all of the clusters that are&nbsp;
still processing tasks around or if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:55.840 --> 01:32:59.760
doing something like video encoding for long&nbsp;
videos, you don't want to shut off the cluster&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:59.760 --> 01:33:03.440
that's in the middle of encoding a long video&nbsp;
because that work would have to be redone.

01:33:04.560 --> 01:33:08.000
In a regular deployment of clusters&nbsp;
that only be shut off after all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:08.000 --> 01:33:10.080
of their long running jobs are done processing.

01:33:12.000 --> 01:33:16.080
Some teams rely heavily on manual QA and&nbsp;
don't use continuous deployment. They're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:16.080 --> 01:33:20.000
often building desktop or mobile apps, which&nbsp;
needs to be published on longer release cycles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:21.040 --> 01:33:26.800
So if example.com is being routed to the blue&nbsp;
cluster, it would be relatively simple to deploy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:26.800 --> 01:33:32.400
a new version of the application to the green&nbsp;
cluster endpoints new.example.com. to that. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:32.400 --> 01:33:36.960
with this setup, the new version of the app could&nbsp;
be tested against the production database in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:36.960 --> 01:33:41.600
very environment that will soon become production.&nbsp;
Such tests are often called acceptance tests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:41.600 --> 01:33:46.720
because they're happening in production with&nbsp;
production data, with nothing like no privileged&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:46.720 --> 01:33:53.520
access to the code base. So for a game, you might&nbsp;
have the new release available, or the API's for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:53.520 --> 01:33:58.777
the new release available and have your QA testers&nbsp;
test that. And after the QA testers give the Go&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:58.777 --> 01:34:04.560
ahead, you can point the game client to the new&nbsp;
version, and then switch the labels of the to

01:34:07.760 --> 01:34:13.520
another useful add on to bluegreen deployments.&nbsp;
And really deployments in general is called the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:13.520 --> 01:34:19.200
canary deployment. So if the new version of your&nbsp;
app contains subjective changes, such as editing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:19.200 --> 01:34:24.720
the UI, it might be ill advised to push them&nbsp;
to all users at once. Facebook has billions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:24.720 --> 01:34:30.080
of users. So if even 1% of their users complained&nbsp;
about a change, that would be overwhelming amounts&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:30.080 --> 01:34:36.560
of feedback. The changes may break users workflows&nbsp;
and need to be modified or rolled back in response&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:36.560 --> 01:34:42.640
to user feedback. So in the context of a bluegreen&nbsp;
deployments, a canary deployment would be to an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:42.640 --> 01:34:48.480
extension which routes maybe 5% of user traffic to&nbsp;
the new version of the application. And check that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:48.480 --> 01:34:53.440
those users don't have negative feedback before&nbsp;
switching the rest of the users over. So if blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:53.440 --> 01:35:00.320
was version one green was version two, we'd have&nbsp;
95% going to version one. 5% going to version two

